# =============================================================================
#  TypR tutor  ·  Lesson 3 of 13  ·  Break it on purpose
# =============================================================================
#
#  The compiler's job is to say NO to code that would fail at run time.
#  Before learning to please it, let's see it at work.
#
#  Below is a small typed function. 'fn(x: int): int' reads: "a function that
#  takes an int called x and returns an int".
#
#  In plain R, twice("two") would only blow up when the line runs, maybe in
#  production, maybe months later. In TypR it never gets that far.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  >>> EXERCISE   (an inverted one: this time, success means an error!)
#
#  This program compiles fine. Add a line at the end that calls 'twice' with
#  a value of the wrong type, so that the compiler REJECTS the program with a
#  type error. Then run:   typr tutor check
#
#  Read the error message carefully: it says what was expected, what it
#  received, and where. Every error has a code (like T001); the tutor shows
#  a longer explanation for it.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

let twice <- fn(x: int): int {
  x * 2
};

print(twice(21));
